ime since they were introduced in  the second half of the 18th century they  have no serious rivals as formats for  organizing social political and economic  life the status that i impute to peace  democracy and free markets in the 21st  century is captured by a story I tell in  the book about a French architect who  was asked where in Paris he would most  like to live he replied the Eiffel Tower  on the grounds that this was the only  place in the city where he wouldn't have  to look at it well that story raises two  points that are germane to this book  first the story represents an important  feature of the book which is that it is  written to be reader friendly it  reflects my conviction that serious  books about politics economics history  and foreign policy and the ideas that  conquered the world covers all of them  should be works of literature written  and clear lively vivid fashion and not  works of science written in a  specialized language intelligible only  to a very few so the reader of the ideas  that conquered the world will find lots  of metaphors a few jokes and no jargon  the second point on which this story  bears is that this is where the person  who is in effect responsible for our  being here in this room comes into the  picture for peace democracy and free  markets were Woodrow Wilson's ideas he  presented them at Paris after World War  one as the formula for organizing the  world in the wake of that terrible  conflict and these three ideas are the  source of Woodrow Wilson's enduring  relevant  to our public affairs as well as to the  ambivalence indeed the sharply polarized  opinions that people continue to hold  about them for Woodrow Wilson did not  succeed in implementing these three  great ideas after World War one the  interwar period was characterized by  anything but peace democracy and free  markets and yet ultimately now in the  21st century as I detail in the book  these ideas have triumphed so Woodrow  Wilson goes down in history at least in  my book as a poor statesman but an  excellent prophet and I say why and how  these are true in the ideas that  conquered the world well to return to  its thesis the second word in this  thesis the triumph of our liberal ideas  is our ideas that is to say who are we  here I mean not just the United States  these three great ideas were invented  primarily in Great Britain and  secondarily in France and so they are  certainly Western in origin but they're  no longer only Western now they are  universal Japan is a market economy even  as Great Britain is India like the  United States is a democracy and this  Universal quality of these three great  ideas is an important source of their  appeal it's not necessary for any  country to side with the United States  on all issues or even on any issue to  embrace these three great ideas it's  perfectly possible to be pro-democracy  and anti-american a certain country in  Western Europe noted for its fashion and  cuisine comes to mind in this regard  well the third qualification involves  the term liberal ideas what do I mean by  that I do not use the term liberal in  the way that it is used in the everyday  discourse of American politics in which  liberals oppose conservatives over  issues such as abortion and taxation  rather I use the term liberal in its  classic original 19th century sense for  liberty in this sense Democrats  Republicans and independents alike in  the United States are all liberals well  the ideas that conquered the world is  divided into three parts the first  involves history the second foreign and  security policies the third politics and  economics and I'd like to say a brief  word about each first history the  purpose of this section is to fit the  present moment into the history of the  last 200 years there's much to say on  this subject but here I'd like to stress  two points in particular first I see  modern history as a battle of ideas  about how to organize and manage the  world in the 19th century that pitted  that battle pitted liberal modern ideas  on the one hand that is peace democracy  and free markets against the traditional  ways that had held sway over most of the  planet for centuries according to which  war if not necessarily desirable was  certainly normal kings and emperors  ruled on the basis of the principle of  heredity and economic life was destined  to be forever stagnant these traditional  ideas lost ground steadily during the  19th century and were swept away by  World War one but in their wake a  different set of ideas opposing  liberalism arose I call these ideas  illiberal ism so as to group together  under a single rubric both fascism and  communism these two 20th century  ideologies differed from each other in  important ways but they did have three  major elements in common both glorified  war both practiced government by  self-appointed dictatorships and both  practiced extensive in the case of  communism almost total government  control  all of Economic Affairs fascism was  defeated in World War two Orthodox  communism in Europe was swept away at  the end of the Cold War leaving the  three great liberal ideas for the first  time without a serious rival and this  leads me to the second point in part one  of the ideas that conquered the world  that I'd like to stress here in part one  I offer a reinterpretation of the Cold  War the Cold War was like the other  great conflicts of the modern period the  Napoleonic Wars at the end of the 18th  and beginning of the nineteenth  centuries and the two world wars of the  20th century all of them were global  struggles the outcome of which  transformed the world but the Cold War  was unlike these predecessor conflicts  in one important way the Cold War was  one not by force of arms but by force of  example it was the success of the West  the success of our liberal political  systems our societies and above all our  economies that were the key to the  collapse of communism and this point is  important for understanding not only the  past but the present and the future as  well for the Western and now Universal  liberal example remains potent the  forces that brought down communism are  still operating they exert a  gravitational pull on the whole world  well the second part of the ideas that  conquered the world is entitled the  invention of peace the main point here  is that in Europe at the end of the 20th  century there arose almost by accident  an answer to an age-old question the  question of how in a world of sovereign  independent states without any world  government to keep order it is possible  to have not just an armed truce which is  after all familiar throughout history  but something deeper and more lasting  genuine peace  the formula for peace at which europe  and the european powers which  emphatically includes the United States  arrived at the end of the 20th century  has two parts both of them involving  armed forces first armed forces in  Europe are configured for defending but  not for attacking territory second all  armed forces in Europe are subject to  transparency according to which every  government can know at every moment just  what forces every other government  deploys and what those governments are  doing with them these two principles  have bred a kind of confidence that  underpins a deeper peace than Europe has  ever known and the key to adopting them  is a cluster of attitudes involving the  use of force that evolved in the West  although not unfortunately as yet  elsewhere over the course of the second  half of the 20th century which I call  war lessness by war lessness I mean a  pronounced principled and widespread  aversion to armed conflict in a chapter  entitled the cure for cancer I described  the origins of what I title the common  security order in Europe and in the  following chapter entitled the most  dangerous place on the planet I assess  the prospects for establishing  comparable security arrangements in the  other part of the world in which a  conflict that could attain global  dimensions could start namely East Asia  now in recent days attention in East  Asia has been focused on the Korean  Peninsula and the prospect of the spread  of nuclear weapons to the regime  controlling the northern part of that  Peninsula this is indeed an important  issue in one with which I deal in the  ideas that conquered the world but the  central issue for the future of East  Asia is the disposition the foreign  policy and the relations with its  neighbors of China here I find good news  and bad news  the good news is that in my judgment  there is only one issue over which China  might begin a war that could embroil  many other important countries and that  issue is the status of Taiwan the bad  news is that at this point there is no  solution in sight to Taiwan status that  is satisfactory to all parties concern I  do not believe that war over the status  of Taiwan is inevitable or even likely  but it is possible and because such a  war could involve two important  nuclear-armed countries the People's  Republic of China and the United States  I call East Asia in general and the  Taiwan Strait in particular the most  dangerous place on the planet now peace  is plainly not universal in the wake of  the Cold War and at the outset of the  21st century how do i account for that  well I begin by dividing the world into  two categories the first category is the  core which consists of the rich powerful  countries of Western Europe North  America and Japan in which new ideas and  new products are invented the core  enjoys unprecedented peace the second  category is the periphery and it  includes almost all other countries the  members of what we call during the Cold  War the third world these are countries  to which new products and ideas diffuse  and this is a part of the world that is  turbulent indeed in some ways more  turbulent in the wake of the Cold War  than during that gre