eriphery is  one of the central facts of the world of  the 21st century but to this  disconnection there is one major  exception and this exception is the  subject of a separate chapter in the  ideas that conquered the world there is  one part of the  planet from which emanate real threats  to the core that part is the Middle East  the Middle East is home to the world's  largest deposits of oil the cut off of  which would inflict serious damage on  core countries including our own the  Middle East is also home to regimes that  define their interest in ways  antithetical to those of the west and  the United States and are seeking  weapons of mass destruction to pursue  those interests Iraq is the obvious  example and the Middle East as we learn  to our sorrow and our cost on September  11 2001 is the breeding ground for the  kind of terrorism that can and has  reached North America the solution to  the manifold problems in the Middle East  and in other countries of the periphery  is the establishment in these countries  of peace democracy and free markets what  are the chances that this will happen in  the course of the 21st century that is  the subject of part 3 of my book the  title of this section is the liberal  theory of history a phrase I've coined  to refer to a widely held if seldom  systematically articulated view that  there is a relationship among these  three great liberal ideas that they go  together that one tends to lead to or at  least foster the others put this way  this sounds almost too good to be true  but the fact that something sounds too  good to be true doesn't necessarily mean  that it's not true and in fact in part  three of the ideas that conquered the  world I find considerable evidence in  favor of two propositions the first of  these is that democracies tend to  conduct peaceful foreign policies at  least toward one another this  proposition has been the subject of  considerable research and discussion in  the field of political studies 