ve a tendency to move to the Western  core countries there by enriching those  countries and penalizing the countries  that they are leaving there are I think  two hopes that arise from this pattern  for these peripheral countries one is  that these countries will recognize what  is happening and act on it that is to  say that they will organize themselves  in such a way as to make it attractive  for talented people to stay the second  hopeful development is that we do see  signs of diasporas of peripheral  countries in the core organizing  themselves abroad to try to have some  impact on their home countries we see  something of this kind happening among  indian americans in the united states  who are becoming more active in the  affairs of india and let us hope that  they will be effective in helping to  nurture some of the liberal values and  institutions in the country to which  they have come in the country that they  have left on david shambo's points it is  of course true that market economies are  not necessarily democracies but I think  that we see in East Asia a hopeful  tendency of countries with  well-established well-functioning free  markets ultimately to adopt liberal  politics we've seen that pattern in  Taiwan and South Korea in the  Philippines and that is after all the  great hope for China and China is the  great test of the liberal theory of  history the hope and indeed this is the  basis for American and Western policy is  that as China  as a more deeply rooted and better  articulated market economy this will in  this will encourage liberalizing  tendencies in Chinese politics and we  know and as David and his colleagues  have written that china is a vastly  different place even politically today  than it was during the heyday of the  Maoist era and the most important reason  for the changes in Chinese society and  Chinese politics the greater personal  freedom that individual Chinese and joy  is I think the creation of a working  free market t