n  Iraq will be enough to galvanize this  so-called anti hegemonic coalition  that's why i say as i as i mentioned in  my prepared remarks that to me an equal  danger is that the united states will  fail to play a sufficiently robust role  in the world odd as that may sound at  this juncture in our history it seems to  me that american reticence is at least  as great a danger to these three great  liberal ideas as an overbearing american  foreign policy now on the subject of  declining support for democracy and free  markets here let me make just two points  first democracy and especially free  markets are not without problems and  indeed free markets because they are as  the great Austrian American economist  Joseph Schumpeter called them exercises  in creative destruction because they  destroy even as they create the workings  of free markets especially the effective  working of free markets is bound to  create a backlash there will always be a  backlash against free markets wherever  they are established and the politics of  countries such as ours will revolve  around the battles between the winners  and the losers or rather they will  involve the battle to decide how to deal  with how to cope with how to compensate  people for the destructive effects of  free markets that is inevitable that is  the center of our politics if we are  lucky a second point about  dissatisfaction with democracy and free  markets is I think relevant here  one of the great strengths that these  ideas and the institution's to which  they give rise possess that I emphasized  in the book is that for the first time  since they were introduced there are no  alternatives to them however  dissatisfied people in Brazil or  Argentina or Indonesia or South Korea  maybe with the workings of the free  market they have nowhere else to go that  was emphatically not the case during the  worst crisis of free markets and  democracies in the modern period namely  the 1930s when capitalism seemed to fail